          "replacement_function_source": "// Applied rules: [III④]\n// Skipped rules: [C3: Hit [0] is only a single loop-carried byte load in a sentinel scan with no repeated invariant field/address-chain reload to hoist; the profitable C3 shapes (S1 signature lift or S2 local hoist) do not fit well here. Any benefit is subsumed by the III④ local slice/CStr view, which removes the raw-pointer cursor loop entirely without cross-function signature change.; C4: Although hit [2] matches a byte-cursor loop, this is a NUL-sentinel scan with no explicit physical end pointer. C4's safety gate requires proving a full 8-byte in-bounds window before each word read; sentinel termination alone does not establish that bound. III④ can safely rewrite this site via `CStr::from_ptr` using the existing NUL-termination contract, so prefer that structural rewrite and abstain from speculative word-at-a-time widening.]\n#[inline]\npub unsafe fn xmlCharStrdup(mut cur: *const c_char) -> *mut xmlChar {\n    if cur.is_null() {\n        return ::core::ptr::null_mut::<xmlChar>();\n    }\n    let len = {\n        // SAFETY: after the null check, `cur` is non-null; by the function's existing contract it points to a NUL-terminated C string within a single allocation; this shared view does not create mutable aliases.\n        unsafe { ::core::ffi::CStr::from_ptr(cur) }.to_bytes().len()\n    };\n    return xmlCharStrndup(cur, len as c_int);\n}",
